<!--Post: 12244--><!--Time: 1111799356-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12244"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 5:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The states charges: 10 counts Murder in the First Degree. Each count a Class A Felony. <BR> <BR><a href="http://news.findlaw.com/cnn/docs/btk/karader30105inf.html" target=_top>http://news.findlaw.com/cnn/docs/btk/karader30105i nf.html</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12244-->
<!--Post: 12253--><!--Time: 1111806779-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12253"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 7:12 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It&#39;s good to see.  I&#39;m surprised by how short the witness list is.  Is this normal?  Will they add more witnesses later?  It seems strange. <BR> <BR>As an aside, I hope they have seen the victim name error in Count Seven, and have taken action to prevent this from happening again.  &#39;Nuff said.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12253-->
<!--Post: 12256--><!--Time: 1111808503-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12256"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 25, 2005 - 7:41 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->At this point and time in the game it is preliminary. However, don&#39;t forget about mandatory disclosures/discovery. I&#39;m certain that witness list will grow siginificantly with 10 victims.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12256-->
<!--Post: 12409--><!--Time: 1112190204-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12409"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 5:43 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->In Kansas mandatory disclosure is limited to exculpatory evidence or statements made by the defendant. <BR> <BR>At some point the DA has to file a motion to &#34;endorse&#34; additional witnesses.  As long as the defense isn&#39;t surprised or prejudiced, such motions are almost always granted. <BR> <BR>The DA is probably advising the Public Defender of the actual witness list by letter to keep the names private until the preliminary hearing. <BR> <BR>I doubt that we’ll see any additions to the witness list until the day before the hearing. <BR> <BR>Phoenix <BR> <BR> <BR>   <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12409-->
<!--Post: 12416--><!--Time: 1112201489-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12416"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 8:51 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Phoenix - BTW I was just in Phoenix a couple of weeks ago, big place! As far as witnesses I&#39;m referring to material, impact and character. According to the attached article the DA&#39;s office hasn&#39;t give much to the defense team. <BR><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_packages/btk/11240220.htm" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_pack ages/btk/11240220.htm</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12416-->
<!--Post: 12421--><!--Time: 1112205606-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12421"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsewhere<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsewhere<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 10:00 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Okay - is it just me?  I read that article on Kansas.com - and it seems to me that the Public Defender Osburn contradicts himself... <BR>in regard to the evidence first he is quoted as saying: <BR>&#34;We haven&#39;t seen a drop in the bucket&#34; <BR>then he is quoted: <BR>&#34;It is just massive - we have seen a lot of it acutally&#34; <BR>Am I reading it wrong?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12421-->
<!--Post: 12443--><!--Time: 1112232286-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12443"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 5:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Else - Your right! He does contradict himself. Phoenix - can you look at that article and explain it to us lay people? &#40;or those of us that don&#39;t work in a criminal practice&#41;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12443-->
<!--Post: 12445--><!--Time: 1112235902-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12445"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 6:25 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The explanation is that it&#39;s a REALLY BIG bucket.  Each drop is massive because they drop into a REALLY BIG bucket.  I&#39;m not sure where the &#39;criminal practice&#39; comes from.  I have friends who are defense lawyers, but I asked them and here&#39;s their take. Each homicide case has common elements. <BR> <BR>Death of the victim:  This requires evidence of the victim&#39;s identity. <BR> <BR>Death by criminal means:  Pathologist on the cause of death as not a heart attack, etc.  Testimony from EMS and other health care providers about therapy they provided.  &#40;It&#39;s not a bruise, it&#39;s CPR&#41; <BR> <BR>CSI personnel:  Evidence from the scene.  How it was stored/marked. <BR> <BR>DNA storage during a time when LE didn&#39;t know about DNA contamination. <BR> <BR>Exclusion Evidence: Who else were suspects?  Each investigative lead is evidence that someone else commited the crime.  This case is FULL of &#34;other suspects,&#34; at least 1,300 if the number of swabs is accurate. The case details of each becomes relevant. <BR> <BR>What did WPD do to confirm/deny that the 1,300 people/swabs were/were not the killer. <BR> <BR>Victim impact testimony from the family. <BR> <BR>DNA evidence &#40;small samples&#41; from 1970&#39;s to now is testified to. <BR> <BR>It&#39;s HUGE.  10,ooo pages could be a &#34;drop in the bucket&#34; <BR> <BR>Whew!!!  I&#39;m going for the Excedrin. <BR> <BR>Phoenix <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12445-->
<!--Post: 12450--><!--Time: 1112238237-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12450"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 7:03 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->So, deriving from your posts&#39; the DA&#39;s office will have to disclose everying and DTDC will only have to disclose evidence that may exclude &#40;exculpatory&#41; himself from one or more of the charges. And of course the Judge gets to rule on what is and isn&#39;t admitted into evidence. So in all multiply by 10 = 10 death certificates, 10 autopsies, 10 different crime scene collections of evidence, etc... Whew - give me some of that Excedrin. &#40;I&#39;m thinkin of all the trial notebooks that would have to be put together&#41; How much did Wichita budget for this case? <BR> <BR>I&#39;ve worked on civil cases that were litigated for 10 years or longer, and sometimes it could be insane when the pre-trial motions came in on rulings of evidence. It does suck when you have to go through each and every trial notebook and either add or remove pages of evidence. Fortunately, most of the evidence in this case will be physical. Thanks Phoenix, your right a drop in the bucket.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12450-->
<!--Post: 12451--><!--Time: 1112240582-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12451"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 7:43 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I read today that Rader is pleading not guilty, this case is starting to make me nervous, First, they are going to try to throw out DNA then the confession then say there was no way he could have done this by himself.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12451-->
<!--Post: 12456--><!--Time: 1112251782-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12456"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 10:49 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Maybe they can get him for breaking and entering...or...maybe leaving his Christmas lights up too long.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12456-->
<!--Post: 12459--><!--Time: 1112254351-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12459"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 11:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->OMG B I forgot about that lol Love E. ROFLMAO<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12459-->
<!--Post: 12465--><!--Time: 1112263198-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12465"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 1:59 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I can understand that excusionary evidence would have to be made available to the defense as a matter of the discovery process, and that the defense would want to challenge anything that they feel excludes other suspects beyond a reasonable amount of suspicion using questionable logic, but I doubt that they are going to make a federal case of each one.  <BR> <BR>The gazillion DNA swabs are not of evidence subsequent to obtaining his daughter&#39;s DNA and should stand as &#39;90% plus change&#39; reliable evidence that the other &#34;three thousand dude&#34;, at least didn&#39;t masterbate at the scene. The only thing &#40;semen-wize&#41; that is in jeapardy is that Denny DID. <BR> <BR>After reading Beattie&#39;s book &#40; and there is still some question to the actual sequence of events in the last week or so&#41; the actual &#34;thing&#34; that led the WPD to suspect Rader was the security video of him circling the block to make his last drop. <BR> <BR>They HAD to suspect him specifically before obtaining his daughters DNA because...and, this will be on the test...they had to know who&#39;s daughter&#39;s DNA to obtain. <BR> <BR>Since they knew who he was before the DNA was obtained, and since there are lawyers involved, I think that &#40;even if they hadn&#39;t made the diskette connection yet&#41; that they would list ALL probable cause on the search warrant for the house and the arrest warrant for Denny. <BR> <BR>Remember, also, that they had to show probable cause to obtain a warrant to obtain her DNA &#40;what was omitted was a protective order from a judge, which to my understanding is to place the medical records in the custody of the court [rather than the prosecution] thereby preventing the disclosure to anyone other that those who must see the evidence, from accessing the entire record&#41;. <BR> <BR>By issuing the warrant, the court acknowleged that there was probable cause to assume that Rader was BTK. Even if the [protective order] court is a higher/different court than the [search/arrest warrant] court, I doubt that one court is gonna call the other court a &#34;liar&#34;. As long as the evidence on the disk and at Rader&#39;s house is not based exclusively on Kerri,s DNA match with the crime scene DNA, the DA should still have all the evidence collected from the church and house to use. Plus it seems, then, that once arrested they could swab Denny and use THAT DNA match in court. <BR> <BR>Of course Kerri can always sue the DA&#39;s office for violating her civil rights, but that won&#39;t get Denny out of jail.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12465-->
<!--Post: 12466--><!--Time: 1112263705-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12466"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 2:08 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I forgot to mention...The aforementioned legal maneuver it known as the &#34;34th Street&#34; precedent or &#34;The Old Santa&#39;s Bag Trick&#34;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12466-->
<!--Post: 12471--><!--Time: 1112272588-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12471"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 4:36 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Embersage: <BR> <BR>Roughly 90% 0f defendants plead not guilty.  Of the remaining 10%, 9%  plead guilty before trial to gain some advantage. <BR> <BR>If defense lawyers earn their salt, they try the 1% remaining. <BR> <BR>Phoenix <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12471-->
<!--Post: 12478--><!--Time: 1112286882-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12478"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 8:34 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I would plead not guilty as well. He has nothing to lose and he likes to see the state go through its gyrations. He watched LE wander aimlessly for most of his SK career. If I were the defense, I would go over him neurologically with a fine tooth comb. His writings hint of problems in that area. He had to have some instigators in his family life. All it would take is a spanking with his pants pulled down to pair pain and a secret sexual thrill. He&#39;s hooked. I&#39;d look at his body physiology/brain chemistry, psychiatric exam, neuropsychological testing, family history for epilepsy/familial dysfunctions, chromosome abnormalities, brain hemisphere dominance/sighting dominance/hand dominance, parents were first cousins, developmental history &#40;did he walk w/o crawling, was he clumsy/accident prone?&#41;, and present the sum as Millay wrote: With him for a sire and her for a dam, what should I be but just what I am. <BR> <BR>Likewise, the prosecution needs to have these bases covered as well. If he pleas out, we may never know the full facts on all the cases, particularly if the defense makes silence as part of the plea agreement. A trial will hang his family out to dry. I doubt that he would be concerned with this, the upside being the attention that he will be receiving.  The waiting game....<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12478-->
<!--Post: 12480--><!--Time: 1112290319-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12480"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 9:31 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The one thing he has going against him is time... <BR> <BR>Denny doesn&#39;t have any problem that he hasn&#39;t been able to control, when it was to his advantage, for at least the last 30 years. And if it is true that he hasn&#39;t killed since the 80&#39;s, like he wants the prosecution to believe, then he&#39;s cured. <BR> <BR>Interesting problem. His Insanity plea would hold more water if he could produce evidence that he had not stopped killing, which if it failed to get him declared insane, would make him eligable for the croak penalty.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12480-->
<!--Post: 12482--><!--Time: 1112291630-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12482"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsewhere<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsewhere<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 9:53 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->There is one legal aspect in all this that I don&#39;t understand.  Here goes...The media has indicated that Rader &#34;confess to six of the murders&#34; and then I could have sworn I read somewhere that he &#34;confessed to all ten&#34; that he is being charged for.  What I don&#39;t get is how can now per his assigned attorney&#39;s turn around and plead &#34;not guilty&#34; after confessing????? <BR>I don&#39;t know alot about the &#34;legal aspects&#34; of all this. <BR>Thanks!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12482-->
<!--Post: 12505--><!--Time: 1112319416-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12505"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 5:36 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->It would seem that one can confess to whatever one would want. I guess that is different from the formal proceedings and an &#39;official&#39; on the record plea. And Bmod is correct. His cunning and manipulation and game playing all indicate his ability to strategize, plan with forethought, and deceive all who knew him, except one dog. The very fact that he sought to avoid detection indicated that he knew his acts were wrong criminally, according to the laws of man, at least. And that is all the court will address. <BR> <BR>Surely there is an attorney on board. One from long ago has been absent.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12505-->
<!--Post: 12509--><!--Time: 1112322815-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12509"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Megabyte<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Megabyte<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 6:33 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->KAKE is airing an interview with Rader&#39;s Pastor, who talks about his discussions with Dennis Rader. <BR><b>Megabyte</b><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12509-->
<!--Post: 12525--><!--Time: 1112365164-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12525"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 6:19 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Whether a defendant knew his conduct was wrong is an issue in an insanity defense.  The court will also decide if DR’s confession can be used against him. <BR> <BR>The potential issues on the confession are: <BR>1.  Was the arrest of DR based on insufficient probable cause? <BR>2.  Was the PC for his arrest obtained in violation of DR’s constitutional rights? <BR>3.  Was the PC for his arrest obtained in violation of statutes? <BR>4.  Was DR improperly mirandized? <BR>5.  Did WPD prevent DR from talking to his attorney during interrogation? <BR>6.  Was his interrogation so lengthy &#40;almost 24 hours&#41; that it constituted coercion? <BR>7.  Did DR suffer from a mental illness that rendered him unable to understand and knowingly waive his Miranda rights? <BR> <BR>If the answer to any of these is yes, the confession may be inadmissible.  The way DR asks the court to examine the constitutionality of his confession is by pleading Not Guilty.  &#40;A plea of guilty waives any defect in the evidence.&#41; <BR> <BR>In Kansas, a defendant does not enter a plea until his arraignment &#40;after the preliminary hearing.&#41; <BR> <BR>At arraignment a defendant may plead:  <BR>1.  not guilty, <BR>2.  guilty, <BR>3.  &#34;No Contest&#34; [The prosecution then tells the court what their evidence would be at trial.], <BR>4.  Or an defendant may stand mute.  [The court then enters a not guilty plea for the defendant.] <BR> <BR>As a practical matter, without plea negotiations, a defendant rarely pleads guilty.  There is nothing to lose by going to trial.  <BR> <BR>Phoenix <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12525-->
<!--Post: 12528--><!--Time: 1112369088-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12528"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tulip<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tulip<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 7:24 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Also take a look at the thread on Deanna Law.  Read the link provided there.  It&#39;s a good example of how a Defendant can be aquitted even when a confession is given and admitted into evidence. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12528-->
<!--Post: 12554--><!--Time: 1112419546-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12554"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 9:25 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->b: <BR> <BR>VERY nice analysis of the issue.  If the court issued the warrant, the defense is on the ropes: not down for the count, but in serious trouble. <BR> <BR>There is no indication that the court issued a warrant or subpoena.  The order sealing the file complicates this issue, and the warrant could be present and just not show up in the record. <BR> <BR>Phoenix <BR> <BR> <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12554-->
